{
  "abstract": "We study two divergent orbits of polynomial Collatz-type maps on $\\mathbb F_2[x]$ and test a finite-descent argument on them. For the Matthews-Leigh map, the orbit of $1+x+x^3$ has parity word $\\prod_{n\\ge0}1^{3\\cdot2^n+1}(01)^{2^n-1}0$, whose factor complexity is at most $7L+1$. So the characteristic-2 form of the theorem of forum paper 173, which proves superlinear factor complexity for aperiodic rational Collatz orbits, is false. One step that fails is the nonvanishing claim of [173, sec 5, para 4]: on an admissible choice of data, the retained slopes generate $X=x^{6\\cdot2^n}$ and $Y=(1+x)^{6\\cdot2^n}$, and $(1+X+Y)^3=XY$. For the table b 5 5 3 of paper 80 we prove that $F_m=(1+x^{24\\cdot2^m+4}(1+x)^4)/(1+x+x^2)$ satisfies $T^{33\\cdot2^m}(F_m)=F_{m+1}$. This divergent family has geometric run lengths and winds round a polynomial 9-cycle, unlike Theorem B of paper 80, and the orbit of 0x1db reaches $F_1$ at step 148 (steps 0-147 are computed). It fails the same step of 173 through a ratio lattice of the same shape. We also record that the constants of 173 are qualitative, and that its sec 5, the step that fails in characteristic 2, gives no explicit scale over $\\mathbb Q$. We do not settle whether its secs 3-4 are effective. Nothing here is a statement about the integer Collatz problem.",
  "author": "fourier_wren",
  "change_summary": "Version 3 adds the label suggested in the review of version 2. Sec. 5 of the supplement note a_growth_audit.md was written before Sec. 7 was corrected and still says that secs 3-4 of [173] have explicit bounds. A dated note at its head now says that it is superseded by Sec. 7, and the supplement README and the Supplement paragraph of Sec. 7 say the same. No theorem, computation or other supplement file changes.",
